  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in 2023 by Peter Lang Verlag as part of the established \"Travail et Société / Work and Society\" series, this book by Andrea Maria Locatelli and Cecilia Maria Bravi addresses labour as a central—yet historically underestimated—dimension of post-World War II economic and social development in Western democracies. The work aligns with the United Nations Sustainable Development Goal on decent work and economic growth, and has begun circulating in academic and institutional collections.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Christian Democracy and Labour after World War II enters a scholarly field that has long marginalized labour's role in shaping both democratic institutions and capitalist development. The authors examine how Christian Democratic-oriented parties and associations conceptualized and promoted labour policy during the second half of the twentieth century, situating these visions within the broader tension between democratic and republican principles and economic expansion. This focus addresses a documented gap: economic and social theory have underestimated labour's constitutive importance to Western industrialized societies.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book's placement within the \"Travail et Société\" series—now in its mid-80s volumes—positions it within a sustained, multilingual conversation on work, employment policy, and social change in Europe. Recent companion volumes in the series examine related topics including trade union organization, workplace diversity, pension reform, and austerity's implementation. This positioning suggests the work participates in an established scholarly dialogue rather than operating in isolation.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Early circulation is documented in academic library systems, with holdings recorded in WorldCat and cataloged under the subjects \"Europe, history\" and \"Social policy.\" Italian research repositories have registered the work, indicating awareness within at least one national scholarly infrastructure. The book carries a persistent DOI and is retrievable through standard academic discovery channels, establishing a stable foundation for future scholarly engagement.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">As a recent monograph in the humanities and social sciences, this work operates within a publication cycle where citation-based impact typically accumulates slowly and unevenly. The absence of early citation signals reflects the typical trajectory for books rather than evidence of limited relevance. Its substantive focus on labour's relationship to democratic governance and economic development, combined with its alignment with Sustainable Development Goal 8 (decent work and economic growth), positions it as potentially relevant to scholars and policymakers engaged with post-war European political economy, labour history, and Christian Democratic thought.</p>",
